menu "Argus"

config ARGUS_LED_GPIO
    int "User LED GPIO"
    default 21
    help
        The XIAO ESP32S3 user LED is GPIO21 and is active low.

config ARGUS_BUTTON_GPIO
    int "Mode button GPIO"
    default 0
    help
        GPIO0 is the BOOT button on the XIAO ESP32S3, active low.  Holding it
        while running toggles the console SoftAP; it only enters download mode
        when held across a reset, so reusing it here is safe.

comment "Wi-Fi uplink -- leave blank here and set it with menuconfig or NVS"

config ARGUS_WIFI_SSID
    string "Home network SSID (optional)"
    default ""
    help
        Leave this EMPTY in sdkconfig.defaults, which is a tracked file.  Set
        it with `idf.py menuconfig` (it lands in sdkconfig, which is
        gitignored), or with a gitignored credentials.conf, or at runtime from
        the console -- which stores it in NVS and never touches the repo at
        all.  A credential set at runtime takes precedence over this one.

        tools/check_no_secrets.sh fails if a value ends up in a tracked file.

config ARGUS_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "Home network password (optional)"
    default ""
    help
        Empty means an open network.  Same warning as the SSID above: never
        commit a real value.

config ARGUS_WIFI_AUTOJOIN
    bool "Join the configured network at boot"
    default y
    help
        When credentials are configured, join at boot so the console is
        reachable on your LAN without the SoftAP.

        This costs detection: the radio has one channel, so while associated
        the Wi-Fi sniffer is suspended and only BLE scanning and AP scans
        continue.  Turn this off to stay on full patrol and reach the uplink
        only on demand.  If the network cannot be joined, Argus falls back to
        patrol rather than sitting associated to nothing.

config ARGUS_UPLINK_RETRY_S
    int "Seconds between attempts to regain the uplink"
    default 300
    range 30 3600
    help
        When the network is configured, uplink is the resting state.  After a
        failed join or a lost association Argus patrols instead, and retries
        the uplink on this interval so it rejoins by itself when you come back
        into range -- which is what delivers any notices queued while away.

        Choosing patrol with the button suppresses this until you select
        uplink again: an explicit choice is not second-guessed.

config ARGUS_UPLINK_GRACE_S
    int "Seconds to tolerate a dropped uplink before patrolling"
    default 45
    range 5 600
    help
        Brief drops are normal and reconnect on their own.  Past this, staying
        in uplink mode means the Wi-Fi sniffer is off and there is no network
        either, so patrolling is strictly better.

config ARGUS_WIFI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Uplink connect timeout (seconds)"
    default 15
    range 5 60

config ARGUS_BLE_SCAN_INTERVAL_MS
    int "BLE scan interval (ms)"
    default 160
    range 20 1000

config ARGUS_BLE_SCAN_WINDOW_MS
    int "BLE scan window (ms)"
    default 60
    range 10 1000
    help
        Must not exceed the interval.  BLE and Wi-Fi share one radio and the
        coexistence arbiter divides it by this duty cycle, sharply and
        non-linearly: at a 100% duty cycle the Wi-Fi sniffer receives almost
        nothing at all (2 frames in 40 s, measured), while 37.5% still yields
        around 200.  The default trades roughly a third of BLE throughput for
        a sniffer that functions.

config ARGUS_AP_SSID_PREFIX
    string "Console SoftAP SSID prefix"
    default "console"
    help
        The last three bytes of the MAC are appended.  The default deliberately
        does not advertise what the device is.

config ARGUS_AP_PASSWORD
    string "Console SoftAP password"
    default "argus-console"
    help
        Must be at least 8 characters for WPA2; a shorter value leaves the
        SoftAP open.  Change it.

endmenu
